{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.049,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.849,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": "If both numbers are doubled, then both numbers get an extra factor of 2 in their prime factors. This 2 will be included as a factor in the largest common subpart, and so the HCF will double. For example, consider the numbers 270 and 50."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.172,
            0.575,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c}270 = \\boxed{2} \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times \\boxed{5} \\\\50 = \\boxed{2} \\times 5 \\times \\boxed{5}\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.238,
            0.516,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{HCF} = 2 \\times 5 = 10",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.266,
            0.62,
            0.286
          ],
          "content": "Let us double these numbers to get 540 and 100."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.377,
            0.293,
            0.624,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}540 = \\boxed{2} \\times \\boxed{2} \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times \\boxed{5} \\\\100 = \\boxed{2} \\times \\boxed{2} \\times 5 \\times \\boxed{5}\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.359,
            0.563,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{HCF} = 2 \\times 2 \\times 5 = 20.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.386,
            0.846,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "？ Consider the following two multiples of $14 - 14 \\times 6, 14 \\times 9$ What is their HCF?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.431,
            0.846,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "Clearly, 14 is a common factor. Is it also the highest common factor? To see it, let us calculate the prime factorisations."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.398,
            0.48,
            0.604,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}14 \\times 6 = \\boxed{2 \\times 7} \\times 2 \\times \\boxed{3} \\\\\\underbrace{14 \\times 9} = \\boxed{2 \\times 7} \\times 3 \\times \\boxed{3}\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.411,
            0.574,
            0.586,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{HCF} = 14 \\times 3 = 42.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.619,
            0.846,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": "？ Here are some more numbers where both numbers are multiples of the same number. Find their HCF:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.67,
            0.348,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": "$1 8 \\times 1 0 , 1 8 \\times 1 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.419,
            0.671,
            0.451,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": "(b)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.463,
            0.67,
            0.614,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": "1 0 \\times 3 8 , 1 0 \\times 2 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.695,
            0.327,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "(c) $5 \\times 1 3 , 5 \\times 2 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.419,
            0.695,
            0.616,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "( \\mathrm { d } ) \\quad 1 2 \\times 1 6 , 1 2 \\times 2 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.724,
            0.847,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": "In which of these cases is the HCF the same as the common multiplier, like problem (b) where the HCF is 10? Explore a few more examples of this type to understand when this happens."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.813,
            0.615,
            0.835
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Efficient Procedures for HCF and LCM"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.84,
            0.711,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "See the procedure on the right. Can you explain how it has been carried out?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.934,
            0.187,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "60"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
